{
  "v": 1,
  "contract": "1.4",
  "about": "normalise(url, canonical?) from contract section 4.3. Every port must produce expect exactly; expect null means the input is refused as not a link. d is kefli/item/ followed by the lowercase hex sha256 of the UTF-8 of expect.",
  "rulings": [
    "Settled on 6 October 2026 with contract 1.4 (section 11.9), Richard having left these to the engineer's judgement. These cases pin choices the contract text leaves open:",
    "1. Parsing and serialisation follow the WHATWG URL Standard (the whatwg-url package on every engine), so default ports, dot segments, IPv4 forms and percent-encoding come out as a browser's location.href would give them.",
    "2. Anything other than http and https is refused.",
    "3. Query names are matched and sorted in their decoded form, as URLSearchParams reads them, but kept byte for byte; matching is case-sensitive, and the sort is stable, so repeated names keep their order.",
    "4. Empty query pairs are dropped, and a query left empty loses its question mark.",
    "5. s and t go on twitter.com, x.com and their subdomains.",
    "6. Every trailing slash is removed, so normalise(normalise(x)) is normalise(x); a path of slashes only becomes /.",
    "7. The canonical link is used only for the alternate forms, and only from the page's own site (ruling 11); a canonical that isn't an http or https URL is ignored, and the URL is normalised as given.",
    "8. User info is kept.",
    "9. A trailing dot on the host is kept, as the URL Standard keeps it, so example.com. and example.com give different d tags.",
    "10. A host written longer than 1,024 characters, its port included and its user info not, is refused before parsing: no host DNS can resolve comes near that length, and UTS-46 on a long Unicode label takes time that grows with the square of its length.",
    "11. Settled on 14 October 2026 with contract 1.12 (section 11.1): a canonical link counts only from the page's own site, so a page at amp.evil.example can't take another site's address and land on, or replace, a save of it. Its host must be the page's host, or equal the page's host once a leading m. or amp. label is taken off the page's and then a leading www. off each, provided the page's host so shortened still has a dot in it (m.com is no mobile form of com). Hosts are compared in their ASCII form, without their ports, and a trailing dot makes another host (ruling 9). Any other canonical is ignored, and the URL is normalised as given."
  ],
  "cases": [
    { "name": "already normal", "url": "https://example.com/article", "expect": "https://example.com/article" },
    { "name": "scheme and host lowercased", "url": "HTTPS://EXAMPLE.COM/Article", "expect": "https://example.com/Article" },
